<Base title="How GPUfit estimates fit and speed" description={description} path="/how-it-works">
  <article class="wrap prose" style="padding:2rem 0 1rem; max-width:46rem;">
    <p class="kicker">Method</p>
    <h1>How it works</h1>
    <p>Every number on GPUfit is an <strong>estimate for llama.cpp-style inference</strong>. It is not a benchmark. The speed line is a range on purpose. If your measured tokens per second disagree, the row on the home page can store that note locally.</p>

    <h2>Memory</h2>
    <p>A model fits when this total stays inside the usable pool:</p>
    <pre class="cmd">total = weights + KV cache + engine overhead</pre>
    <p><strong>Weights.</strong> When Hugging Face lists a GGUF file, we use that file’s byte size (split shards are summed once). The catalog prefers Unsloth, then bartowski, then LM Studio community builds. If no file exists yet, the size is <code>parameters × bits-per-weight / 8</code> and the row is marked estimated. The bits-per-weight table is approximate llama.cpp behavior: Q4_K_M is 4.85, Q5_K_M is 5.7, Q6_K is 6.56, Q8_0 is 8.5, F16 is 16. Those constants live in <code>src/data/quant-meta.json</code> and are shared by the fetcher and the estimator.</p>
    <p><strong>KV cache.</strong> For ordinary grouped-query attention the cache is FP16:</p>
    <pre class="cmd">bytes = 2 × layers × KV heads × head dim × 2 × context</pre>
    <p>The layer count, KV heads, and head dimension come from the model’s <code>config.json</code> (the text config, when a vision model wraps one). Three cases change the formula:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sliding window and hybrid attention</strong> (Gemma, some Qwen). Only full-attention layers keep the whole context. Sliding layers keep <code>min(context, window)</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>MLA</strong> (DeepSeek and similar). The cache stores the compressed latent, <code>(kv_lora_rank + qk_rope_head_dim) × 2</code> bytes per layer per token, which is several times smaller than a wide GQA cache.</li>
      <li><strong>Linear attention / Mamba layers.</strong> Their state does not grow with context. When the config publishes the linear head sizes, state is <code>layers × value heads × value dim × key dim × 2</code> bytes. Otherwise we use a flat 2 MB per linear layer and say so.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Engine overhead.</strong> The verdict uses llama.cpp’s published starting point from VRAM Calculator: 0.3 GB plus 1% of the weights. Ollama is closer to 0.9 GB + 3%, and LM Studio closer to 1 GB + 3%. Those larger engines can push a “tight” fit over the edge. We do not currently switch the verdict per engine.</p>
    <p><strong>Usable memory.</strong> Device capacity is binary: a “16 GB” GPU is 16 × 1024³ bytes, which is what the driver reports. File sizes are exact bytes and are displayed in that same GiB scale, labeled GB.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Discrete GPU that drives a monitor: subtract 0.5 GB, then leave 5% free.</li>
      <li>Datacenter and headless cards skip the display reserve. Turn the display checkbox off on the home page if the card is not driving a screen.</li>
      <li>Apple unified memory, and other unified parts such as Ryzen AI Max: the GPU budget is 75% of RAM, also capped by RAM minus about 5 GB for the OS. The 75% figure is the default documented by soverignplan, not a firmware limit. If the model fits only after that cap is lifted, the verdict is “Raise memory limit”.</li>
      <li>CPU-only: keep 4 GB plus another 10% for the OS. Bandwidth is the theoretical dual-channel rate, for example DDR5-5600 = 89.6 GB/s.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Verdicts</h2>
    <p>Bands follow llmfit’s documented pool usage, applied to the usable memory above. llmfit is MIT licensed; we use the thresholds, not their code.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Runs great</strong> — at or under 60% of the usable pool.</li>
      <li><strong>Runs</strong> — at or under 85%.</li>
      <li><strong>Tight fit</strong> — at or under 98%.</li>
      <li><strong>Expert offload</strong> — a mixture-of-experts file does not fit, but a rough shared-layer slice does, and system RAM can hold the experts. The split is approximate.</li>
      <li><strong>Partial offload</strong> — some transformer layers stay on the GPU and the rest sit in system RAM.</li>
      <li><strong>Runs on CPU</strong> — the device has no discrete GPU.</li>
      <li><strong>Won’t fit</strong> — even the smallest sensible quant does not fit in GPU memory plus system RAM.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The recommended quant is the highest-quality published file that still leaves at least 4K of context (or the model’s whole context, if that is shorter). Speed is quoted at 8K context when that fits, because quoting the maximum context makes the tokens/sec number look worse than a normal chat.</p>

    <h2>Speed</h2>
    <p>Decode reads the active weights and the KV cache for the current context on every token:</p>
    <pre class="cmd">tok/s ≈ utilization × bandwidth / (active weights + KV at that context)</pre>
    <p>Utilization is shown from 0.55 to 0.75 of the vendor’s peak bandwidth. Ridgepoint measured about 0.61 on vLLM; 0.65 is the middle of our range. Mixture-of-experts models use <code>active / total</code> of the file for the bandwidth read, while <em>memory</em> still counts the whole file, because every expert has to be stored. If layers spill to RAM, time is GPU bytes over GPU bandwidth plus CPU bytes over RAM bandwidth.</p>
    <p>Prefill, speculative decoding, concurrent requests, and KV-cache quantization are not in this version. Multi-GPU is not modeled; pick one card.</p>

    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Model list, parameter counts, and GGUF sizes: the public Hugging Face API. A token is optional and only raises rate limits. See <code>scripts/fetch_models.py</code>.</li>
      <li>Architecture: each base model’s <code>config.json</code>.</li>
      <li>Ollama names: one gentle fetch of the public library HTML, used only to print <code>ollama run &lt;name&gt;</code>. If the page shape changes, the command is omitted.</li>
      <li>Hardware: a hand-checked table in <code>src/data/hardware.json</code>, generated from <code>scripts/hardware_catalog.py</code>. Each row has a source URL. NVIDIA 50-series bandwidths are from NVIDIA’s compare table. Apple M4 and M5 figures are from Apple’s newsroom and MacBook Pro specs. CPU bandwidths are computed from the transfer rate. We do not scrape TechPowerUp.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The scheduled job is <code>.github/workflows/model-watcher.yml</code>. It reruns the fetch every six hours and commits <code>models.json</code> when the catalog changes. A model is “new” when its repo was not in the previous file. Junk uploads are dropped unless they come from a trusted quantizer or clear a download bar, and the page filter also drops diffusion, embedding, and speech repos.</p>

    <h2>What is not calibrated yet</h2>
    <p>There is no fitted correction from llama-bench. The home page can remember a speed you type, in this browser only. Publishing those measurements, and comparing them with the LocalLLaMA explorer dataset (CC-BY-4.0), is future work. Until then, treat a “20–30 tok/s” line as a bandwidth ceiling, not a promise.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
